---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-to-one-line">
  <DevFormatJson client:load mode="oneline" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>One line, still readable</h2>
    <p>
      To minify and to collapse JSON to one line are different operations. Minifying removes all
      optional bytes and gives <code>&#123;"id":42,"name":"Ada"&#125;</code>. Collapsing keeps one
      space after each colon and comma and gives <code>&#123;"id": 42, "name": "Ada"&#125;</code>. That
      is still one line and still valid, and much easier to read in a log viewer or a terminal at 2am.
      With the spacing switch off, the two are identical.
    </p>

    <h2>Where a single line is required</h2>
    <h3>Log lines</h3>
    <p>
      Structured logging pipelines (journald, Docker&rsquo;s json-file driver, Fluent Bit, Vector,
      CloudWatch) treat a newline as a record boundary. A pretty-printed JSON payload in a log becomes
      fifteen unrelated records, and none of them parse. A search for the request ID finds only the line
      that has it. One line per event makes the log queryable.
    </p>
    <h3>Environment variables</h3>
    <p>
      A newline inside an env var is possible but awkward in almost all runtimes: Docker
      <code>--env</code>, systemd <code>Environment=</code> and most CI secret stores reject it or
      require escaping. Collapse the JSON first to avoid the problem.
    </p>
    <h3>Command-line arguments</h3>
    <p>
      You can put a one-line payload in single quotes and pass it to curl:
      <code>curl -d '&#123;"a": 1&#125;' -H 'content-type: application/json' …</code>. If the JSON
      contains a single quote, use <a href="/json-escape">JSON escape</a> to make a fully escaped
      literal, or write the body to a file and use <code>-d @file.json</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>Spreadsheet cells and issue trackers</h3>
    <p>
      Pasting multi-line JSON into a spreadsheet cell splits it across rows. One line pastes as one
      cell.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens to newlines inside strings</h2>
    <p>
      They stay escaped. A string with a real line break is invalid JSON, so in any input that parses,
      newlines are stored as the two-character sequence <code>\n</code>. Collapsing does not change
      string contents, so a multi-line message field stays the same. Only the layout between tokens
      changes.
    </p>

    <h2>Going back</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing is lost, so the <a href="/json-formatter">JSON formatter</a> turns the one-line result
      back into an indented document. Key order stays the same unless you sorted it, so the round trip
      is exact.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
